alse,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":3,"created_at":"2025-11-27T23:53:04Z","updated_at":"2025-11-29T08:54:35Z","closed_at":"2025-11-29T08:54:27Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nv4.5.rc2.mono.official [2dd26a027]\n\n### System information\n\nLinux\n\n### Issue description\n\nI have animations that need to set property values for attacks to enable and disable hitboxes.\n\nWhen I add a boolean property track that defaults to false, keyframe true at 1.0, keyframe false at 2.0, I would expect the boolean to be false until 1.0 seconds into the animation - at which it turns true.\n\nWith `force_continuous` set this is not the case. It appears to use the first keyframe value immediately, and will be set true until it is set false later into the animation.\n\nI believe this is some quirk of using `force_continuous`, but I don't understand why the animation mixer cannot default to the keyframe value found inside the reset track.\nFrom my understanding `force_continuous` uses the nearest value for keyframes, and the first keyframe is the nearest value, but I would love for it to use reset track values.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Create AnimationPlayer node\n2. Attach a script with an exported boolean defaulting to false\n3. Create an animation, setting the boolean to true half-way through\n4. Set animation mixer to use `force_continuous`\n5. Run the game\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\npress left click to begin playing the animation, notice it prints true immediately.\n[testing-property-track.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/23811901/testing-property-track.zip)","closed_by":{"logi